From 8010b9ecbe52862ae2daa3536c27ca259592117e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rafe Walker <45343084+rafe-walker@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 12:03:35 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 02/19] feat(core): apply without force by default and report
 field conflicts; add create-only mode (C24, C26)

resources_create_or_update force-applies every manifest. Combined with
its description's recipe ("fetch it with resources_get, modify it, then
re-apply the full resource"), that loses concurrent changes: on the
native session (job 17) another writer changed MAX_CONNECTIONS between
the agent's read and its write, the agent's full copy took the field
back to the old value with force, the tool answered success, and the
agent told the user the other keys were unchanged. Without force the
API refuses such a write with a field-ownership conflict.

Changes:
- Server-side apply is no longer forced by default. A conflict is
  returned as a FieldConflictError that names the object and each
  conflicting field with the manager that owns it, and says how to
  proceed: send only the fields you mean to change, or pass
  `force: true` to take them over. Nothing is written for that
  document.
- `force: true` restores the previous behaviour.
- `mode: create` (C26) creates each object with a POST instead of
  applying it. An existing object of the same name is never modified;
  the error names it and its field managers. Without it, avoiding an
  overwrite costs a read before every create (job 16), and a careless
  agent overwrites.
- pods_run and vm_create keep forcing, on objects they have already
  checked are their own (or new).

Trade-off: forcing is a deliberate upstream choice (#799, H-04), meant
to make re-applies of the tool's own manifests never fail. With this
change a re-apply that touches fields another manager has since taken
fails until the caller drops those fields or passes force: true. That
is the point of the change, but it is a behaviour change, and
upstream would see it as a proposal, not a fix.

TestResourcesCreateOrUpdateForcesSSA asserted the forced takeover; it
now passes force: true. New tests cover the refused stale copy (the
other writer's value is kept), a minimal manifest that succeeds next to
another manager's fields, and mode: create.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
